.TH RUSTGRAPH 1 "July 2026" "rustgraph 0.8.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rustgraph \- AST\-aware Rust codebase navigation built for AiDX
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rustgraph
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.PP
.B rustgraph find
.RB [ \-\-func | \-\-struct | \-\-enum | \-\-const | \-\-trait | \-\-alias ]
.I QUERY
.br
.B rustgraph callers
.RB [ \-C
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-depth
.IR N ]
.I FN
.br
.B rustgraph ensemble
.RB [ \-\-view
.IR V ]
.RB [ \-\-preset
.IR P ]
.I FN
.br
.B rustgraph paths\-between
.RB [ \-\-show\-call\-sites ]
.I FROM
.I TO
.br
.B rustgraph mcp
.RB [ install | uninstall | list | serve ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBrustgraph\fR is an AST\-aware Rust source navigator. It parses Rust
crates with \fBsyn\fR(3), builds a symbol index and a call graph, and
exposes both through a CLI, a Rust library, and a Model Context
Protocol (MCP) server.
.PP
The tool is designed AiDX\-first \(em "AI Developer Experience". When
the caller is a model, every redundant token costs money and every
false positive in \fBgrep\fR(1) wastes a turn. Output, errors,
defaults, and help text are shaped so an agent can finish a navigation
task in one call instead of six. Humans get a fast, deterministic CLI
as a side effect.
.PP
Because resolution happens on the parsed AST, results do not match
string literals, comments, attribute arguments, or unrelated tokens
that share a name. For raw text search across those surfaces, use
\fBrustgraph grep\fR (documented below) which still skips non\-Rust
files.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These flags are accepted before any subcommand. Most are
.IR global ,
i.e. accepted in any position. Run
.B rustgraph \-\-help
for the full reference.
.SS Resolution
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-path\fR \fIPATH\fR
Crate root path (default \fI.\fR). Cargo.toml auto\-discovery walks
upward from this path.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-path
Disable Cargo.toml auto\-discovery; use the value of
.B \-p
verbatim.
.TP
.B \-\-absolute\-paths
Emit absolute file paths instead of paths relative to
.BR \-p .
.TP
\fB\-\-also\fR \fIPATH\fR
Extra crate root(s) to merge for cross\-crate analysis. Repeat to add
more than one.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-resolution
Drop call edges where type\-strict matching returned 0 candidates
instead of falling back to bare\-name match. Reduces false positives at
the cost of hiding some legitimate edges. The dropped count is reported
on stderr. Affects \fBcallers\fR, \fBcall\-graph\fR, \fBpaths\-between\fR.
.SS Output
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
Emit JSON with a stable schema; pipes cleanly to \fBjq\fR(1).
.TP
.B \-\-include\-ignored
Include files normally excluded by \fI.gitignore\fR (typically
\fItarget/\fR, \fIbuild/\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Force colored / disable colored output (default off). Mutually exclusive.
.SS Search
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fIQUERY\fR
Fuzzy search across functions, structs, enums, consts/statics, traits,
and type aliases. Use \(lq|\(rq inside the query for OR alternatives.
.TP
\fB\-\-search\-threshold\fR \fIF\fR
Fuzzy match threshold in [0.0, 1.0]. Default \fB0.85\fR; raise toward
\fB1.0\fR for stricter matches.
.TP
.B \-\-match\-signature
Additive: union name\-tier hits with hits whose signature (params /
return) or file path fuzzy\-matches the query (deduped; name wins on
collision). A summary like \(lqnote: \fIX\fR name + \fIY\fR sig/path
matches\(rq is emitted on stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-tests
Drop matches whose enclosing function is a test. Honored by \fBgrep\fR,
\fBrefs\fR, \fBfind\fR, \fBdead\-code\fR, \fBcallers\fR, \fBensemble\fR,
\fBusages\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-public\-only
Filter to public items only (skip \fBpub(crate)\fR and private). Applies
to \fBinventory\fR and \fBfind\fR.
.SS Diff
.TP
.B \-\-changed
Filter to symbols whose enclosing fn/struct/enum was changed since
\fB\-\-since\fR (default \fBHEAD~1\fR). Requires \fBgit\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIGIT_REF\fR
Diff base for \fB\-\-changed\fR. Any git ref (branch, tag, sha).
.SS Inventory mode (no subcommand)
.TP
\fB\-\-analyze\fR \fIMODE\fR
One of \fBfunctions\fR, \fBstructs\fR, \fBenums\fR, \fBconsts\fR,
\fBtraits\fR, \fBaliases\fR, \fBboth\fR (default \(em every kind).
Inventory mode only \(em for lookup use \fBfind\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-func\fR, \fB\-\-struct\fR, \fB\-\-enum\fR, \fB\-\-const\fR, \fB\-\-trait\fR, \fB\-\-alias\fR
Inventory shortcuts: list only the named symbol class(es). Combine
freely.
.SS Help and version
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help. Use \fB\-\-help\fR for the long form.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version.
.SH COMMANDS
Each subcommand accepts the global options above. Unless noted,
list\-producing subcommands take \fB\-n\fR/\fB\-\-max\-results\fR to cap
output (with a footer reporting the original total when truncated) and
\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR to scope to files whose path contains
\fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR.
.SS find
\fBrustgraph find\fR
[\fB\-\-func\fR|\fB\-\-struct\fR|\fB\-\-enum\fR|\fB\-\-const\fR|\fB\-\-trait\fR|\fB\-\-alias\fR]
[\fB\-\-exact\fR] [\fB\-\-show\-ids\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] \fIQUERY\fR
.PP
Short alias for symbol lookup; roughly equivalent to \fB\-\-search\fR
\fIQUERY\fR \fB\-\-search\-threshold 0.85\fR across functions, structs,
enums, consts/statics, traits, and type aliases. Muscle\-memory
replacement for \(lq\fBrg\fR \fI<name>\fR\(rq. \fIQUERY\fR is a single
identifier or \(lq\fI<a>\fR|\fI<b>\fR\(rq alternatives \(em \fInot\fR
\fIpath.rs\fR:\fILINE\fR (use \fBcallers\fR for that) and \fInot\fR a
multi\-word phrase (queries containing whitespace are refused with
steering; use \fBrustgraph grep\fR for text search).
.PP
Exact name matches render above fuzzy ones. When only fuzzy matches
exist, the output leads with \(lqnote: no exact name match for
\fIQUERY\fR\(rq and every fuzzy name hit carries its similarity score
(\fB[name ~0.89]\fR), so a guess is never dressed as an exact hit.
.TP
\fB\-\-func\fR, \fB\-\-struct\fR, \fB\-\-enum\fR, \fB\-\-const\fR, \fB\-\-trait\fR, \fB\-\-alias\fR
Restrict to one or more symbol classes. \fB\-\-const\fR covers consts
and statics (module\-level and impl\-associated).
.TP
.B \-\-exact
Exact (case\-insensitive) name match with no fuzz expansion. Does not
error on 0 or many matches.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-ids
Append \(lq[id: \fIsymbol_id\fR]\(rq to each text\-mode line. Use when
round\-tripping through \fBcallers \-\-symbol\-id\fR /
\fBensemble \-\-symbol\-id\fR (or just pass \fB\-j\fR).
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph find KIND_META \-\-const\fR
.SS def
\fBrustgraph def\fR [\fB\-\-func\fR|\fB\-\-struct\fR|\fB\-\-enum\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] \fINAME\fR
.PP
Go\-to\-definition: print exact symbol location as
\fIpath\fR:\fIline\fR:\fIname\fR. Exits \fB1\fR if 0 or more than 1
match. Use this when \fBfind\fR is too fuzzy and a single deterministic
answer is required. \fINAME\fR matches case\-sensitively.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph def DaemonState \-\-struct\fR
.SS callers
\fBrustgraph callers\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-A\fR \fIN\fR \fB\-B\fR
\fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-flat\fR]
[\fB\-\-target\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR]
[\fB\-\-callers\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-\-symbol\-id\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-ambiguity\-cap\fR \fIN\fR] \fIFN\fR
.PP
List functions calling \fIFN\fR. \fIFN\fR accepts a name,
\fIpath.rs\fR:\fILINE\fR, or a canonical \fIsymbol_id\fR. On ambiguous
queries (more than \fB\-\-ambiguity\-cap\fR matches, default \fB7\fR)
the candidate list is printed and the process exits non\-zero; pass
\fB\-\-all\fR to process them anyway, or disambiguate with
\fB\-\-target\-in\fR or \fB\-\-symbol\-id\fR.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-B\fR, \fB\-C\fR \fIN\fR
Trailing / leading / symmetric context lines around each call site
(default \fB0\fR). \fB\-C\fR overrides the individual flags when set.
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Caller chain depth. Default \fB1\fR (direct callers); set \fB2+\fR for
transitive chains; \fB0\fR for unlimited (cycle\-safe). Output gains a
depth field per node and a tree view in text mode.
.TP
.B \-\-flat
Emit all transitive callers as a deduplicated
\fIpath\fR:\fIline\fR:\fIname\fR list (no tree). Pipe\-friendly.
Mutually exclusive with \fB\-j\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-target\-in\fR / \fB\-\-callers\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR
Filter target functions (\fB\-\-target\-in\fR) or caller functions
(\fB\-\-callers\-in\fR) by file\-path substring. \fB\-\-callers\-in\fR
filters at every depth.
.TP
\fB\-\-symbol\-id\fR \fIID\fR
Resolve target by exact \fIsymbol_id\fR (from \fBfind \-j\fR or
\fBcallers \-j\fR). Bypasses fuzzy matching. When set, \fIFN\fR is
optional.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph callers \-C 2 \-\-depth 2 build_ledger\fR
.SS usages
\fBrustgraph usages\fR [\fB\-\-kind\fR \fIKIND\fR]...
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] \fINAME\fR
.PP
Combined \(lqcallers \fI<name>\fR\(rq + \(lqrefs \fI<name>\fR\(rq. One
stop for "what touches this name" when you do not yet know whether
\fINAME\fR is a function (use \fBcallers\fR) or a type/field (use
\fBrefs\fR). \fB\-\-kind\fR filters the refs half only (same values as
\fBrefs \-\-kind\fR); the callers half is unaffected. Default cap
\fB100\fR. Also exposed over MCP as \fBrustgraph_usages\fR.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph usages SellOrder \-\-kind struct\fR
.SS refs
\fBrustgraph refs\fR [\fB\-\-kind\fR \fIKIND\fR]... [\fB\-\-by\-function\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] \fIIDENT\fR
.PP
Find every reference to \fIIDENT\fR: type uses, field reads, struct
construction, pattern matches, method calls, plain paths — including
inside macro bodies (\fBvec![...]\fR, \fBassert_eq!(...)\fR), whose
token streams are re\-parsed with exact line numbers. Complement to
\fBcallers\fR (which only walks function\-call edges). Matched by exact
last\-segment name.
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fIKIND\fR
Filter by classification tag. Allowed: \fBfield\fR, \fBpath\fR,
\fBassoc_call\fR, \fBvariant\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBstruct\fR, \fBpattern\fR,
\fBmethod\fR, \fBcall\fR, \fBmacro\fR. Repeat to allow multiple.
\fBvariant\fR matches
enum\-variant qualifiers (e.g. \fIColor\fR::\fIRed\fR); \fBassoc_call\fR
matches associated functions (e.g. \fISessionDocument\fR::\fIload\fR);
\fBmethod\fR matches \fI.method()\fR calls regardless of receiver type;
\fBmacro\fR is the best\-effort ident hit inside a macro body that does
not re\-parse as expressions or statements (exact line, approximate
classification).
\fBassoc_call\fR and \fBvariant\fR filter on the \fIqualifier\fR
(Type/Enum), not the leaf name.
.TP
.B \-\-by\-function
Group references by enclosing function (similar to
\fBgrep \-\-by\-function\fR).
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph refs SymbolContext \-\-kind type \-\-kind assoc_call\fR
.SS members
\fBrustgraph members\fR [\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] \fITYPE\fR
.PP
List every access site for each field of \fITYPE\fR. Closes the gap
where \(lqrefs \fI<Type>\fR \-\-kind field\(rq returns \fB0\fR because
\fB\-\-kind field\fR filters on the field \fIname\fR, not the owner
type. Each field's site list is truncated independently (default
\fB50\fR per field); the header counts stay un\-capped. For enums, use
\(lqrefs \fI<Enum>\fR \-\-kind variant\(rq instead.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph members ProjectSummary\fR
.SS impls
\fBrustgraph impls\fR [\fB\-\-derived\-only\fR | \fB\-\-handwritten\-only\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] \fITRAIT\fR
.PP
List every type that implements \fITRAIT\fR, both via
\fB#[derive(Trait)]\fR and hand\-written \fBimpl Trait for X\fR blocks.
Resolves the trait name by its last path segment, so \fBSerialize\fR
matches \fBserde::Serialize\fR; pass the fully qualified name to
disambiguate.
.TP
\fB\-\-derived\-only\fR / \fB\-\-handwritten\-only\fR
Restrict to derive\-generated or hand\-written impls.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph impls Serialize \-\-handwritten\-only\fR
.SS paths-between
\fBrustgraph paths\-between\fR [\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-show\-call\-sites\fR]
[\fB\-\-target\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR]
[\fB\-\-to\-module\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] \fIFROM\fR [\fITO\fR]
.PP
Aliased as \fBwhy\fR. DFS through the call graph to enumerate paths
from \fIFROM\fR to \fITO\fR. Answers "does \fIA\fR actually reach
\fIB\fR, and through what?". Both arguments accept a name,
\fIpath.rs\fR:\fILINE\fR, or \fIsymbol_id\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum path length in nodes (default \fB8\fR; \fB0\fR for unlimited).
High values explode combinatorially in dense graphs.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-max\-results\fR \fIN\fR
Cap on the number of paths emitted (default \fB10\fR; \fB0\fR for
unlimited).
.TP
.B \-\-show\-call\-sites
Annotate each hop with the source line where the next call happens.
First lookup wins when a function calls the same callee from multiple
sites.
.TP
\fB\-\-to\-module\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR
Blast\-radius mode: search to \fIany\fR function whose file path
contains \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR, not just one \fITO\fR. When set, \fITO\fR is
optional. If both are given, the destination set is the union.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph paths\-between handle_request build_budget \-\-show\-call\-sites\fR
.SS ensemble
\fBrustgraph ensemble\fR [\fB\-\-view\fR \fIV\fR] [\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIP\fR]
[\fB\-\-section\fR \fIS\fR]... [\fB\-\-lifecycle\-types\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-boundaries\fR] [\fB\-\-boundaries\-min\-score\fR \fIF\fR]
[\fB\-\-target\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-\-symbol\-id\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-ambiguity\-cap\fR \fIN\fR] \fIFN\fR
.PP
Build a function context bundle: source code, structs touched, call
sites, neighborhood (upstream + downstream), value\-flow hints, type
lifecycles, and I/O boundaries. One ensemble call typically replaces
4\(en6 grep + read cycles. Functions only \(em for structs/enums use
\fBdef\fR or \fBfind\fR and read the reported range.
.TP
\fB\-\-view\fR \fIV\fR
High\-level output view: \fBsummary\fR (default; structs, call\-sites,
neighborhood, dataflow with abbreviated neighborhood), \fBusage\fR
(call\-sites + neighborhood), \fBflow\fR (neighborhood + lifecycle +
dataflow + boundaries), or \fBfull\fR (every section, often 300+ lines).
.TP
\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIP\fR
Depth profile: \fBquick\fR (tightest limits), \fBbalanced\fR (default),
or \fBdeep\fR (largest limits, deeper neighborhood, no call\-site cap).
.TP
\fB\-\-section\fR \fIS\fR
Cherry\-pick which sections render. Repeat for multiple. Valid:
\fBcode\fR, \fBstructs\fR, \fBcall\-sites\fR, \fBneighborhood\fR,
\fBlifecycle\fR, \fBdataflow\fR, \fBboundaries\fR, \fBall\fR. Overrides
\fB\-\-view\fR when set.
.TP
\fB\-\-lifecycle\-types\fR \fILIST\fR
Pipe\-separated list of type names to analyze. Defaults to structs used
by the matched function.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-boundaries\fR / \fB\-\-boundaries\-min\-score\fR \fIF\fR
Suppress the I/O boundaries section, or set its minimum confidence
score in [0.0, 1.0]. Default \fB0.7\fR drops noisy 0.5\(en0.65 entries;
\fB0.0\fR for the unfiltered firehose.
.TP
\fB\-\-symbol\-id\fR \fIID\fR
Resolve target by exact \fIsymbol_id\fR; positional \fIFN\fR becomes
optional.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph ensemble handle_request \-\-view full\fR
.SS dead-code
\fBrustgraph dead\-code\fR [\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR]
[\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
.PP
Aliased as \fBorphans\fR. Find public functions with zero non\-test
callers. Skips re\-exported items, items inside \fBcfg(test)\fR,
runtime entrypoints, framework handlers, and ambiguous resolutions.
Pair with \fB\-\-exclude\-tests\fR to also drop test functions
themselves from the audit set.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Show the skip\-reason summary line (re\-exported / inside \fBcfg(test)\fR /
runtime entrypoints / ambiguous / framework\-handler counts). Useful once
for debugging a missed entry.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph dead\-code \-\-in src/daemon \-\-exclude\-tests\fR
.SS call-graph
\fBrustgraph call\-graph\fR [\fB\-\-root\fR \fIFN\fR | \fIROOT\fR]
[\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-callers\fR]
[\fB\-\-detail\fR \fID\fR] [\fB\-\-show\-external\fR] [\fB\-\-dot\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\fR]
.PP
Generate a rooted or full project call graph. Default output is an
indented text tree (terminal\-friendly). Pass \fB\-\-dot\fR for graphviz
output.
.TP
.I ROOT
Anchor function as a positional argument; equivalent to \fB\-\-root\fR
\fIROOT\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fIFN\fR
Anchor the graph at \fIFN\fR (name or \fIpath.rs\fR:\fILINE\fR) and
walk outgoing call edges to emit a subgraph instead of the full project.
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
When \fB\-\-root\fR is set, cap the BFS depth (default \fB3\fR; \fB0\fR
for unlimited). Ignored without \fB\-\-root\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-callers
When \fB\-\-root\fR is set, also include callers of root up to
\fB\-\-depth\fR hops up. Default is downstream\-only.
.TP
\fB\-\-detail\fR \fID\fR
Edge detail: \fBinternal\fR (default; intra\-crate only), \fBexternal\fR
(cross\-crate or unresolved only), or \fBall\fR (every edge).
.TP
.B \-\-show\-external
Show the \fB<external>\fR rollup line listing unresolved callees (std
iterator chains, method calls, cross\-crate fns). Hidden by default in
text mode. No effect in \fB\-\-dot\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Render the whole project graph (no scope filter). Required when no
\fB\-\-root\fR or \fB\-\-search\fR is set on a project with more than 50
functions. Small projects (<= 50 fns) auto\-render without this flag.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph call\-graph main \-\-depth 4 \-\-dot > graph.dot\fR
.SS tree
\fBrustgraph tree\fR [\fB\-\-files\-only\fR] [\fIPREFIX\fR]
.PP
Print the module/file tree of the project, with symbol counts per file.
Replaces \fBls\fR(1) and \fBtree\fR(1) for code navigation. \fIPREFIX\fR
limits the output to a subtree.
.TP
.B \-\-files\-only
Hide symbol counts; show files only.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph tree src/daemon\fR
.SS grep
\fBrustgraph grep\fR [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-F\fR] [\fB\-C\fR \fIN\fR |
\fB\-A\fR \fIN\fR \fB\-B\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-enclosing\-function\fR]
[\fB\-\-by\-function\fR | \fB\-\-by\-file\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH_SUBSTR\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] \fIPATTERN\fR
.PP
Text/regex search across Rust source files. Catches everything
\fB\-\-search\fR does not see (comments, string literals, attribute
args). Skips non\-Rust files.
.PP
Regex flavor is the Rust \fBregex\fR crate \(em not PCRE. No
backreferences or lookarounds; use \fB\eb\fR for word boundaries,
\fB(?i)\fR inline for case\-insensitive (or pass \fB\-i\fR); char
classes \fB[abc]\fR, \fB[^abc]\fR, \fB\ed\fR, \fB\ew\fR, \fB\es\fR and
friends. See
.UR https://docs.rs/regex/latest/regex/#syntax
.UE
for the full grammar.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fR / \fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-fixed\-string\fR
Case\-insensitive match / treat \fIPATTERN\fR as a literal string.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-B\fR, \fB\-C\fR \fIN\fR
Trailing / leading / symmetric context, identical to \fBcallers\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-enclosing\-function
For each match, also report the enclosing function name and its
\fIfile\fR:\fIstart\fR\-\fIend\fR range. Folds the grep \(rA fn\-mapping
pipeline into one call.
.TP
.B \-\-by\-function
Token\-saver: skip the per\-line dump, emit only the per\-function
summary table (count desc, with \fB[test]\fR tags). Implies
\fB\-\-enclosing\-function\fR. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-by\-file\fR.
Aliased as \fB\-\-rollup\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-by\-file
Token\-saver: skip per\-line dump, emit only a per\-file rollup
(\fI<path>\fR: \fIN\fR matches). JSON envelope: \fBby_file\fR array of
\fB{path, count}\fR. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-by\-function\fR.
.PP
Default cap is \fB200\fR matches. Always single\-quote the pattern in
your shell to keep \fB$\fR and \fB!\fR literal.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph grep '\eb.unwrap\e(\e)' \-\-by\-function\fR
.SS inventory
\fBrustgraph inventory\fR [\fB\-\-func\fR | \fB\-\-struct\fR | \fB\-\-enum\fR | \fB\-\-const\fR | \fB\-\-trait\fR | \fB\-\-alias\fR]
.PP
Dump every fn/struct/enum/const/trait/alias in the project (parse\-only
baseline). Equivalent to invoking \fBrustgraph\fR with no subcommand and
the same shortcut flags. Useful for piping to \fBwc\fR(1) for symbol
counts.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph inventory \-\-func | wc \-l\fR
.SS mcp
\fBrustgraph mcp\fR [install [\fB\-\-quiet\fR] | uninstall [\fB\-\-quiet\fR] | list | serve]
.PP
MCP (Model Context Protocol) server plus self\-registration. With no
sub\-action, defaults to \fBlist\fR (read\-only registration state).
The \fBserve\fR sub\-action is what AI clients spawn after registration
to talk MCP over stdio JSON\-RPC \(em it is not for interactive human use.
.TP
.B install
Detect installed AI clients (Claude, Codex, Gemini) and register the
rustgraph MCP server in each one's config. Idempotent. Atomic write
with timestamped backup.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the rustgraph MCP entry from every detected client config.
.TP
.B list
Show MCP registration state across detected clients (read\-only). This
is the default action when \fBmcp\fR is invoked with no sub\-action.
.TP
.B serve
Serve MCP over stdio JSON\-RPC. Spawned by AI clients via the
\fBcommand\fR/\fBargs\fR pair written by \fBmcp install\fR. Not intended
for interactive use.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress per\-client status lines on \fBinstall\fR and \fBuninstall\fR.
The exit code still reflects success.
.PP
See \fBMCP SERVER\fR below for the protocol details and the list of
exposed tools.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph mcp install\fR
.SS completions
\fBrustgraph completions\fR \fISHELL\fR
.PP
Generate a shell completion script for \fISHELL\fR on standard output.
Supported shells: \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, \fBfish\fR, \fBelvish\fR,
\fBpowershell\fR.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph completions zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_rustgraph"\fR
.SS generate-man
\fBrustgraph generate\-man\fR [\fB\-\-output\fR \fIDIR\fR]
.PP
Generate this man page (and any subcommand pages) from the live clap
definition and write the \fBgroff\fR source to \fIDIR\fR (default:
standard output). Useful for packagers regenerating \fBrustgraph.1\fR
at build time.
.PP
\fBExample:\fR \fBrustgraph generate\-man \-\-output ./man\fR
.SH MCP SERVER
\fBrustgraph\fR ships an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that
exposes the most common navigation operations as agent\-callable tools
over stdio JSON\-RPC. The server is the same binary; it is spawned by
the client on demand.
.PP
Run \fBrustgraph mcp install\fR to register the server with every
detected client. The install is idempotent and writes atomically; each
modified config is backed up to a timestamped sibling
(\fI<config-file>.rustgraph-bak.<unix-ts>\fR) before the new contents
are renamed into place.
.PP
Detected client config files:
.TP
.B Claude Code
\fI~/.claude.json\fR \(em added under \fBmcpServers.rustgraph\fR with
\fBtype = stdio\fR. Honors \fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR (see
\fBENVIRONMENT\fR).
.TP
.B Codex
\fI~/.codex/config.toml\fR \(em added as a \fB[mcp_servers.rustgraph]\fR
TOML table.
.TP
.B Gemini CLI
\fI~/.gemini/settings.json\fR \(em added under \fBmcpServers.rustgraph\fR
with \fBtrust = true\fR.
.PP
Each entry invokes the binary as \(lq\fBrustgraph mcp serve\fR\(rq. The server
announces itself as \fBrustgraph\fR on the MCP wire and exposes six
tools that map to the highest\-leverage CLI subcommands. Agents should
reach for the MCP tools over text \fBgrep\fR(1) when navigating Rust
because they resolve on the parsed AST and never match string literals
or comments.
.TP
.B rustgraph_find
Locate a Rust symbol (fn/struct/enum/const/static/trait/alias) by name.
AST\-aware. Returns \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR range plus signature plus a
match\-kind tag; fuzzy hits carry their similarity score. Optional
\fBkind\fR and \fBthreshold\fR arguments mirror the CLI filters. Default
fuzzy threshold \fB0.85\fR; auto\-relaxes to \fB0.7\fR on 0 results.
Pick this when the agent needs to know \fIwhere\fR something lives.
.TP
.B rustgraph_callers
List every function calling a target, with call\-site lines and
enclosing\-fn context. Handles \fIType\fR::\fImethod\fR collisions.
\fBdepth: 0\fR = unlimited transitive (cycle\-safe). \fBflat: true\fR =
deduplicated \fIpath\fR:\fIline\fR:\fIname\fR list. Pick this for "who
depends on this fn" questions.
.TP
.B rustgraph_usages
Every AST\-resolved reference to a type, field, or fn: struct literals,
type ascriptions (\fBVec<X>\fR, params, returns, impl blocks), pattern
matches, field accesses, assoc/method calls \(em including inside macro
bodies. Optional \fBkind\fR filter narrows the refs section
(\fBstruct\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBpattern\fR, \fBfield\fR, ...). Pick this
for "where is this \fItype\fR used" / "who constructs \fIX\fR" / "what
breaks if I add a field" \(em questions \fBrustgraph_callers\fR cannot
answer because a struct literal is not a call edge.
.TP
.B rustgraph_ensemble
Function context bundle: structs touched, call sites, neighborhood,
dataflow, I/O boundaries. Views: \fBsummary\fR (default), \fBusage\fR,
\fBflow\fR, \fBfull\fR. Presets: \fBquick\fR, \fBbalanced\fR (default),
\fBdeep\fR. Pick this when the agent needs to \fIunderstand\fR a function
before editing, not just locate it. One ensemble call typically replaces
4\(en6 grep + read cycles.
.TP
.B rustgraph_paths_between
Enumerate distinct call\-graph paths from \fIFROM\fR to \fITO\fR via DFS.
Answers "does \fIA\fR reach \fIB\fR, and through what?". Default
\fBmax_results: 8\fR; pass \fB0\fR for full enumeration.
\fBshow_call_sites: true\fR annotates each hop with the source line.
.TP
.B rustgraph_tree
Module/file tree with per\-file symbol counts (fn, struct, enum, plus
const/trait/alias when present). Optional \fBprefix\fR scopes to a
subtree; \fBfiles_only\fR hides the counts. Replaces \fBls\fR(1) /
\fBtree\fR(1) when the agent does not yet know a symbol name.
.PP
For everything else (def, refs, members, impls, dead\-code,
call\-graph, grep, inventory) the agent should fall back to the CLI on
PATH.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude.json
Claude Code MCP registry. Modified by \fBrustgraph mcp install\fR /
\fBuninstall\fR. Path is overridden by \fBCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR\fR if set;
rustgraph then reads/writes \fI$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.codex/config.toml
Codex MCP registry. Modified by \fBrustgraph mcp install\fR /
\fBuninstall\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.gemini/settings.json
Gemini CLI MCP registry. Modified by \fBrustgraph mcp install\fR /
\fBuninstall\fR.
.TP
.IB <config-file> .rustgraph-bak. <unix-ts>
Per\-config timestamped backup written before any \fBinstall\fR or
\fBuninstall\fR mutation. Safe to delete once the change has been
verified.
.TP
.IB <config-file> .rustgraph-tmp
Atomic\-write staging file, renamed onto the target. Should never
persist; if it does, the previous run was interrupted mid\-write and
the file may be safely removed.
.TP
.I ~/.config/rustgraph/no-nudge
Planned opt\-out marker. When this file exists, periodic
\(lqmcp install\(rq nudges (printed by the CLI when it detects an
installed AI client with no rustgraph entry) are suppressed for the
current user. Touch the file to silence the nudge.
.TP
.I ./Cargo.toml
Crate manifest discovered upward from \fB\-p\fR unless
\fB\-\-no\-auto\-path\fR is set. Drives crate\-root resolution.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Overrides the directory containing \fI.claude.json\fR. When set,
\fBrustgraph mcp install\fR reads and writes
\fI$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json\fR instead of \fI~/.claude.json\fR.
Useful for parallelizing multiple Claude accounts (e.g.
\(lqCLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude\-alt rustgraph mcp install\(rq).
.TP
.B RUSTGRAPH_MISUSE_LOG
Opt\-in diagnostics for the MCP server. When set to a file path, each
failed or zero\-output MCP tool call appends one JSON line (timestamp,
version, cwd, argv, outcome) to that file, letting an operator mine
where agents misfire across projects. Unset or empty (the default),
nothing is ever written \(em no log files, no caches. Best\-effort:
logging errors never affect the tool call itself.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Trace upstream callers of a function with two\-line context:
\fBrustgraph callers \-C 2 \-\-depth 2 build_ledger\fR
.TP
Find dead public functions, scoped to one layer, ignoring tests:
\fBrustgraph dead\-code \-\-in src/daemon \-\-exclude\-tests\fR
.TP
Show every call site of a function, deduplicated and pipe\-friendly:
\fBrustgraph callers handle_request \-\-depth 0 \-\-flat\fR
.TP
Register the MCP server with every detected AI client:
\fBrustgraph mcp install\fR
.TP
Get the full context bundle for one function in JSON:
\fBrustgraph \-j ensemble handle_request \-\-view full\fR
.TP
List every type implementing a trait, hand\-written impls only:
\fBrustgraph impls Serialize \-\-handwritten\-only\fR
.TP
Locate a const table and its exact line range for reading:
\fBrustgraph find KIND_META \-\-const\fR
.TP
Cross\-crate caller lookup against a sibling crate:
\fBrustgraph \-p ../sibling\-crate callers handle_request\fR
.TP
Per\-function rollup of every \fB.unwrap()\fR call in the codebase:
\fBrustgraph grep '\eb.unwrap\e(\e)' \-\-by\-function\fR
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. The query produced output (possibly empty) and no error
occurred.
.TP
.B 1
Generic failure. Most commonly: 0 matches when 1 was required (e.g.
\fBdef\fR), ambiguous match when \fB\-\-all\fR was not passed (e.g.
\fBcallers\fR, \fBensemble\fR), unscoped \fBcall\-graph\fR on a project
with more than 50 functions without \fB\-\-all\fR, parse error in the
target crate, or invalid argument combination.
.TP
.B 2
Argument parse error from clap (unknown flag, missing required value,
mutually exclusive flags both set).
.PP
Diagnostics are written to stderr. Resolution\-quality notes (e.g. "X%
of edges resolved by name fallback", \(lqnote: \fIX\fR name + \fIY\fR
sig/path matches\(rq) are also written to stderr so they do not
contaminate \fB\-j\fR output on stdout.
